{"id": "ans-001", "class": "single", "trap": false, "q": "how many tokens of overlap do consecutive text chunks share when the chunker cuts mid-paragraph", "must_facts": ["Mid-paragraph cuts back up about 48 tokens, so consecutive text chunks share a ~48-token overlap"], "evidence": ["DEFAULT_TOKEN_OVERLAP\\s*=\\s*48"], "nice_facts": ["That is about 11% of a 450-token chunk", "Paragraph-boundary cuts carry no overlap"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/chunker.py:L36-66"], "notes": "single-fact; one chunk answers. v3 pack: 3 records, 3257 tok; evidence DEFAULT_TOKEN_OVERLAP = 48 in the chunker constants chunk + 'back up ~overlap_tokens tokens' in the docstring."}
{"id": "ans-002", "class": "single", "trap": false, "q": "what is the target token size for one text chunk in the goldmine chunker", "must_facts": ["The token-budget chunker targets about 450 tokens per text chunk"], "evidence": ["Target `~450 tokens`"], "nice_facts": ["effective_token_budget shrinks the budget to min(450, ceiling - framing(~40) - slack(24)), floored at 64", "~48 tokens of overlap on non-paragraph cuts"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/01-search-relevance.md:L348-379", "evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/chunker.py:L403-437"], "notes": "single-fact. v3 pack: 2 records, 2906 tok; evidence 'Target ~450 tokens' (01-search §5.5) + DEFAULT_TOKEN_BUDGET / min(450, ceiling - framing(~40) - slack(24)) in the chunker code."}
{"id": "ans-003", "class": "single", "trap": false, "q": "how are embedding vectors stored inside the sqlite index", "must_facts": ["Vectors are stored as 16-bit float (fp16) blobs beside their segment rows", "Vectors live in the same SQLite file as the rows that describe them, so a crash can never leave them out of step", "The whole vector table is loaded once per command"], "evidence": ["fp16 BLOBs beside their rows", "crash can never leave them out of step", "loaded once per command"], "nice_facts": ["DigUp ran the same layout at ~20k vectors / ~30 MB with brute-force scan in a few ms"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/05-architecture-data.md:L68-104", "/Users/yul/git/DigUp/Sources/DigUpKit/IndexStore.swift:L1-36"], "notes": "v3 re-word: the 1536-byte/BLOCK_ROWS-8192 atoms sat in chunks the pack does not retrieve (F1) - replaced with the three storage facts the pack carries. v3 pack: 3 records, 3953 tok."}
{"id": "ans-004", "class": "single", "trap": false, "q": "how many members must a topic cluster have before it can become a distill card", "must_facts": ["Clusters with fewer than min_members members (default 3) are dropped and do not become cards", "Dropped small clusters are reported in the run stats rather than hidden"], "evidence": ["--min-members` \\(default 3\\)", "dropped buckets"], "nice_facts": ["--min-members overrides the default", "the 10 largest dropped clusters' top-3 terms are printed"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/distill.py:L279-323", "evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/06-distill-knowledge.md:L128-162"], "notes": "single-fact. v3 pack: 2 records, 2826 tok; evidence 'still dropped from cards by --min-members (default 3)' + 'dropped buckets ... printed' in 06-distill §4.4."}
{"id": "ans-005", "class": "single", "trap": false, "q": "what journal mode does the goldmine sqlite index use", "must_facts": ["The index connection sets PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL", "WAL is the deliberate, kept-by-decision default policy: one-shot commands rely on SQLite's own crash recovery, and ingest ends with a TRUNCATE checkpoint so backups stay single-file"], "evidence": ["PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL", "keep WAL default"], "nice_facts": ["busy_timeout 60000 and foreign_keys ON sit beside WAL in the storage DDL", "goldmine backup uses VACUUM INTO for a single-file consistent snapshot including WAL content"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/ARCHITECTURE.md:L234-267", "evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/05-architecture-data.md:L684-720"], "notes": "v3 re-cut put the current store.py (connect_index, WAL) into the corpus; the synchronous=NORMAL rationale still is not in the retrieved span (F6), so the why-atom is the recorded WAL policy + checkpoint fact the pack carries. v3 pack: 3 records, 3944 tok; evidence 'PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL' (ARCHITECTURE DDL) + 'WAL policy (decided): keep WAL default' + 'TRUNCATE checkpoint' (05-arch §4.8)."}
{"id": "ans-006", "class": "single", "trap": false, "q": "in the sqlite keyword search, how are matching rows ranked with bm25", "must_facts": ["Keyword matching is an FTS5 OR-match and matching rows are ordered by bm25(fts, 3.0, 1.0) AS rank", "bm25 is lower-is-better: the search orders by it ascending (best first)"], "evidence": ["bm25\\(fts, 3\\.0, 1\\.0\\) AS rank", "bm25 is lower-is-better: ORDER BY ascending"], "nice_facts": ["the fts5 tokenizer is porter unicode61 with diacritics removed"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/store.py:L683-724", "evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/01-search-relevance.md:L135-171"], "notes": "v3 re-word (F1): the name-column-weight phrasing needed a column mapping no retrieved span carries; the question now tests what the pack shows - the exact bm25 call and its direction. v3 pack: 2 records, 3076 tok."}
{"id": "ans-007", "class": "multi", "trap": false, "q": "how does the chunker decide where to cut a text chunk", "must_facts": ["Cut preference is tiered: deepest paragraph boundary first, then sentence end (CJK-aware), then whitespace", "A candidate boundary only counts when it sits at least half the token budget into the window", "If no preferred boundary exists the chunk is hard-cut at the budget's token boundary", "Non-paragraph cuts back up about 48 tokens so the next chunk overlaps"], "evidence": ["paragraph/sentence/whitespace cut", "all >= half the budget into the window", "hard cut at the token boundary", "other cuts back up ~`overlap_tokens` tokens"], "nice_facts": ["CJK sentence ends need no following space", "loc/loc_end are 1-based line numbers of the cleaned text"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/chunker.py:L224-316", "evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/01-search-relevance.md:L348-379"], "notes": "multi-chunk: tier list + half-budget floor + hard-cut fallback + overlap live across the chunk_text_tokens docstring and _walk_tokens; v3 pack: 3 records, 3963 tok."}
{"id": "ans-008", "class": "multi", "trap": false, "q": "what happens when a modality group has fewer than twenty vectors during z-score scoring", "must_facts": ["Groups smaller than 20 vectors use the global mean instead of a per-modality mean for centering", "The standard deviation has a 0.02 floor so a tiny spread cannot blow up the z-scores"], "evidence": ["groups < 20 vectors use the global mean", "std has a 0\\.02 floor"], "nice_facts": ["Called the V3 tiny-group fix, measured on DigUp", "Same rule implemented in DigUp's Searcher"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/search.py:L501-517", "/Users/yul/git/DigUp/Sources/DigUpKit/Searcher.swift:L307-350"], "notes": "multi-chunk: zscores docstring carries both facts verbatim; DigUp Searcher.swift carries the same 0.02 floor in code. v3 pack: 2 records, 3172 tok."}
{"id": "ans-009", "class": "multi", "trap": false, "q": "how does hybrid search decide which files qualify as candidates", "must_facts": ["A file qualifies when its best chunk z-score reaches the z gate (3.0 by default; the v2.0 lexical-anchor arm was absorbed there)", "A file can also qualify through the coverage quorum: at least half (0.5) of the query's words found anywhere in the file", "Hard filters intersect afterwards: the candidate set is intersected with the filter-allowed files"], "evidence": ["absorbed at gate 3\\.0", "cov >= _COVERAGE_QUORUM", "candidates &= allowed"], "nice_facts": ["A same-file wrong-location hit still counts for coverage but scores less in nDCG"], "grader_note": "The material contains an outdated v2.0 architecture diagram showing z gate 2.0 alongside the current rule; the current gate is 3.0 (the code and the v2.1 calibration comment carry it). Grade against the current rule and ignore the stale diagram value.", "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/search.py:L1-20", "evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/search.py:L338-355", "evals/corpus_snapshot/ARCHITECTURE.md:L99-131"], "notes": "multi-chunk: the hybrid candidates line + fusion comments carry the gate/quorum/intersect facts; the ARCHITECTURE diagram is intentionally kept (F10) with the grader note adjudicating the stale 2.0 value. v3 pack: 3 records, 3877 tok."}
{"id": "ans-010", "class": "multi", "trap": false, "q": "what do lookup queries change in the fusion weights and what kind of query counts as a lookup", "must_facts": ["Normal fusion weights are meaning 1.0 and words 2.0; lookup queries invert them to meaning 0.5 and words 4.0", "A query counts as a lookup when ANY term contains a digit and is alphanumeric-mixed or at least 4 digits long (codes/IDs)", "The weights act on one fused score: w_m*max(z, 0) + w_c*coverage^1.5 + kind_hint*1.0 (flat, never stacks)"], "evidence": ["\\(w_m, w_c\\) = \\(1\\.0, 2\\.0\\); lookup queries", "alphanumeric-mixed or >= 4 digits\\) invert to \\(0\\.5, 4\\.0\\)", "w_m\\*max\\(z, 0\\) \\+ w_c\\*coverage\\^1\\.5"], "nice_facts": ["lookup success@1 moved 0.594 -> 0.609 on the n=146 llama eval with no query regressing", "codes like K7Q2LM mean nothing to an embedding but everything to keywords"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/search.py:L1-27", "evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/01-search-relevance.md:L212-241"], "notes": "v3 re-word (F1): the is_lookup docstring (rationale + K7Q2LM examples) is not in the retrieved span; mf3 is now the fused-score formula the module docstring carries. Both fact records are the search.py module docstring + 01-search §5.2. v3 pack: 3 records, 3554 tok."}
{"id": "ans-011", "class": "multi", "trap": false, "q": "what happens when a single chunk is too long for the embedding server's token ceiling", "must_facts": ["An 1800-char code stretch once measured 2670 tokens against the 2048 ceiling and 400-failed its file (the incident that drove the fix)", "The chunk budget shrinks to min(450, max_input_tokens - prompt framing (~40 tokens) - 24 slack)", "A single over-budget stretch is hard-cut at a token boundary (split-not-fail) instead of failing the file", "Before embedding, the ctx guard counts tokens and splits any still-oversized chunk"], "evidence": ["reached 2670 tokens against the 2048", "min\\(450, max_input_tokens − prompt_overhead\\(~40", "split that chunk at a token boundary and continue", "_ctx_guard_split"], "nice_facts": ["Code tokenizes about 7x denser than prose", "tokenize endpoint pieces power token-boundary cuts"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/01-search-relevance.md:L348-379", "evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/chunker.py:L430-438"], "notes": "multi-chunk: incident + budget formula + ctx-guard split across 01-search §5.5 and chunker code. v3 pack: 2 records, 2948 tok; the budget formula uses the U+2212 minus, verbatim from the pack."}
{"id": "ans-012", "class": "multi", "trap": false, "q": "how are code chunks cut differently from prose chunks", "must_facts": ["Code chunks align to whole lines, accumulating lines up to the token budget", "A single line longer than the budget becomes its own chunk, hard-cut at token boundaries", "The estimator converts the token budget with the measured code density (CODE_CHARS_PER_TOKEN) so code stretches are sized correctly", "Prose cuts prefer paragraph/sentence/whitespace boundaries; code has no such preference"], "evidence": ["accumulate whole lines to the same token budget", "single over-budget line is hard-cut at token boundary", "int\\(budget_tokens \\* CODE_CHARS_PER_TOKEN\\)", "tiers paragraph > sentence > whitespace"], "nice_facts": ["measured code density is 0.67 chars per token (vs ~4 for prose)", "DigUp codeStretch=1800 won its eval"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/chunker.py:L281-326", "evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/01-search-relevance.md:L364-379"], "notes": "v3 re-word (F1): the 0.67 constant value is not in the retrieved span; mf3 now tests the conversion mechanism (CODE_CHARS_PER_TOKEN in the estimator path) and the value moved to nice_facts. v3 pack: 2 records, 2824 tok."}
{"id": "ans-013", "class": "cross", "trap": false, "q": "where is the 0.97 near-duplicate cosine threshold used", "must_facts": ["Search folds a hit under a higher-ranked hit when their segment vectors sit within cosine 0.97 (or identical text) - display bookkeeping only, order unchanged", "Distill uses the same 0.97 threshold to drop near-duplicate chunks before clustering", "The search constant is documented as 'SPEC distill near-dup parity': the rule originates in the distill dedupe"], "evidence": ["_NEAR_DUP_COSINE = 0\\.97", "pairwise cosine ≥ 0\\.97", "SPEC distill near-dup parity"], "nice_facts": ["Vectorless segments fold on text identity only", "01-search documents the same rule as a planned 'hard collapse ... pairwise cosine >= 0.97'"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/search.py:L61-101", "evals/corpus_snapshot/docs/v2/01-search-relevance.md:L315-348"], "notes": "cross-document: search.py (applied, 'same text for display purposes') + 01-search §5.4 (origin/parity). v3 pack: 2 records, 2896 tok."}
{"id": "ans-014", "class": "cross", "trap": false, "q": "how do the query prompt and the document prompt differ before text is embedded", "must_facts": ["Queries are wrapped as 'task: search result | query: {text}'", "Documents are wrapped as 'title: {title} | text: {text}'", "Both prompt strings are EmbeddingGemma 2 model-card prompts, verbatim and versioned in the index fingerprint - changing either forces a reindex"], "evidence": ["QUERY_PROMPT = \"task: search result \\| query: \\{text\\}\"", "DOCUMENT_PROMPT = \"title: \\{title\\} \\| text: \\{text\\}\"", "changing either string forces a reindex"], "nice_facts": ["The prompt scheme is versioned v1 in the fingerprint", "backends never apply prompts themselves; callers apply them"], "sources": ["evals/corpus_snapshot/goldmine/backends/base.py:L1-36", "evals/corpus_snapshot/SPEC.md:L156-175"], "notes": "cross-document: backends/base.py constants + the fingerprint-role comment. v3 pack: 3 records, 3810 tok."}
